{"hash":"e2ea4fc737b1fefd","index":21,"payload":{"attempt":0,"kind":"RAW_PROPOSAL","raw":"Zn=1 and Rc=1 both landed on unknown vectors, so I need to jointly marginalize over the two unassigned vectors (not just treat each experiment in isolation). Recomputing: both symbols must have vectors from the surviving \"outcome=1\" set for whichever hypothesis is true, and the two vectors must be distinct (bijection). Re-deriving the pair-counts per hypothesis:\n\n- H01,H06,H08,H11: each only has ONE vector giving outcome=1, so two *distinct* symbols both hitting outcome=1 is impossible \u2192 eliminated.\n- H03,H04,H10,H29: each has exactly 2 satisfying vectors \u2192 2 ordered pairs survive \u2192 weight \u221d 2.\n- H22 (NOT p1 OR p2): true on all 4 vectors \u2192 12 ordered pairs survive \u2192 weight \u221d 12.\n\nTotal weight units = 2+2+2+2+12 = 20, giving posterior: H03=0.1, H04=0.1, H10=0.1, H22=0.6, H29=0.1.\n\nChecking EXPERIMENT(Kr) (still-unprobed symbol) against this posterior: for H03/H04/H10/H29, once Zn and Rc consume the two vectors where that hypothesis outputs 1, the two vectors left over for Kr/Ly both output 0 \u2014 so Kr=1 is *impossible* under those four. Only H22 (true everywhere) can produce Kr=1. So this experiment is maximally diagnostic: outcome=1 \u2192 certainty on H22; outcome=0 \u2192 H22 eliminated, remaining four collapse to uniform 0.25 each.\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"COMMITMENT\",\"hypotheses\":{\"H03\":0.1,\"H04\":0.1,\"H10\":0.1,\"H22\":0.6,\"H29\":0.1},\"action\":\"EXPERIMENT(Kr)\",\"predictions\":{\"outcome=1\":0.6},\"update_kind\":\"REWEIGHT\"}\n```","ts":1787359168.2805781},"prev":"754b8ae1d5f5d884"}
